global economic growth has slowed and  the latest figures from china and u.s  are a bit disappointing  even the imf has predicted a heart year  2015 a difficult year 2015.  what that does that mean i will talk  about this with stefan schneider from  deutsche bank research  mr schneider  what did the dbx do in january  actually the dbx dropped by another 10th  percentage point from 0.4 in december to  0.3 and if we look at the driving forces  it's mainly the us where the ism has  dropped by one and a half  points which is one of the weakest for  the last 12 months  we saw also a small drop in the chinese  indicator whereas the indicators for  europe and japan actually continue to  improve  the falling oil prices are quite good  for the world economy of course  the oil producing countries have  problems with that oil price what kind  of long-term effects does the low oil  price have  i mean it's first of all a good for the  number for the countries we are looking  at i mean these are all  industrialized economies or  big oil importers so they should all  benefit from lower oil prices and some  rules of thumb show that uh decline of  the oil price by some 40 50 percent like  we have seen it to have seen it right  now should add roughly three quarters of  a percentage point of global gdp which  is obviously distributed unevenly the  oil exporters are suffering we can see  that in russia but also other countries  and it might in the medium term have  also an implication for for oil supply  that some of the supply will be taken  out of the market or not come on stream  which means that in the medium term  probably oil prices will recover  somewhat to two levels we have seen  maybe a in the middle of last year  at the moment the  low oil price and is a problem for the  european union inflation rate yes this  inflation rate and the aim to to um  of the  or the ecb is to have an inflation rate  of about two percent what is behind this  magic number of two percent actually  it's a problem people would like to have  because it means strong real income  gains oil prices are down nine percent  on the year this has driven the euro and  inflation rate into negative territory  the most recent reading was minus 0.6 so  i think overall we should not really be  too much worried about the job  we should actually enjoy it and we can  see that consumers are taking advantage  of it we can see that consumer spending  and consumer confidence is picking up  but the ecb is worried about long-term  inflation expectations and the concern  is that the consumers and investors will  basically start thinking that even in  the medium term inflation will be way  lower than the ecb's target which is  slightly  below two percent and that might have an  impact on their spending behavior and it  might also  cause some constraints for monetary  policy so the ecb would like to see  inflation expectations which have most  recently come down a little bit but  which are honestly not so much forward  looking  to to reconverge to around two percent  what we have seen in january was that  the swiss bank the swiss national bank  has abandoned the cap between euro and  the swiss franc what  what why did they  why have they decided about that i mean  the swiss national banks president  jordan made it explicitly clear he said  it was  aimed for a certain adjustment period to  give the swiss industry the expert  orientated industry time to adjust to  the strongest swiss rank and the other  argument of course was  the policy innovation in in europe  meaning that the ecb at the beginning of  january had decided to start a massive  qe program 60 billion per month adding  more than one trillion to the ecb's  balance sheet and of course um stemming  against this one would have caused the  ecb's this this national bank's balance  sheet almost to explode jordan just said  if the inflows  the snb saw just in the first few days  of january if that had continued the  balance sheet would 